Module strap module:19 (Host_Config[0]) is open; module:18 VIO = 3.29 V.
Measured and fixed:
Crossed pairs: on the real hardware, module:39 drives and module:38 receives (two outputs fought, giving 1.1 V lows). Fixed with UART1 DTE mode: UART1_RXD → DTE_TX (som:p93 → module:38), UART1_TXD → DTE_RX (som:p94 ← module:39). Both data lines now swing cleanly 0–3.3 V.
Flow control: turned off (/delete-property/ uart-has-rtscts). GPIO5_IO27 (som:p95) is held low by the hog bt-uart-cts-force-low; GPIO5_IO26 (som:p96) is a plain input. module:36 and module:37 both read low.
BT reset: U-Boot left GPIO4_IO22 (som:p19) driven low. Released by the hog bt-reset-release (input); it reads 3.29 V.
UART1 clock: it ran from the 24 MHz oscillator (base_baud = 1500000), so 3 Mbaud was impossible. Added assigned-clocks = <&clk IMX8MP_CLK_UART1>; assigned-clock-parents = <&clk IMX8MP_SYS_PLL1_80M>; plus #include <dt-bindings/clock/imx8mp-clock.h>.
Driver behaviour:
At boot, btnxpuart always says FW already running, then -110 timeouts. The bootloader signature isn’t caught at probe (~10.2 s).
A manual rebind (echo serial0-0 > /sys/bus/serial/drivers/btnxpuart/{unbind,bind}) catches it: ChipID: 7601, Request Firmware: nxp/uartspi_n61x_v1.bin.se.
Before the clock fix this ended in FW Download Timeout. offset: 0. The bootloader then stayed at ~3 Mbaud (about 340 ns bits; the 10-byte A7 firmware-request packets were confirmed by simulation) until a power-cycle, which explains the later Frame reassembly failed (-84) errors.
Latest state (2026-10-08, after the clock-fix dtb and a power-cycle): the rebind showed ChipID and the firmware request, and hciconfig showed DOWN INIT RUNNING. The result after waiting 60 s isn’t known yet.
